===Verb===
{{en-verb|upsets|upsetting|upset}}

# {{senseid|en|to distress}}{{lb|en|transitive}} To make (a person) [[angry]], [[distressed]], or [[unhappy]].
#: {{syn|en|anger|distress|forset|sadden|Thesaurus:enrage|Thesaurus:sadden}}
#: {{ux|en|I’m sure the bad news will '''upset''' him, but he needs to know.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[disturb]], [[disrupt]] or [[adversely]] [[alter]] (something).
#: {{syn|en|disrupt|disturb|forset|turn upside down|Thesaurus:upset}}
#: {{ux|en|Introducing a foreign species can '''upset''' the ecological balance.}}
#: {{ux|en|The fatty meat '''upset''' his stomach.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[tip]] or [[overturn]] (something).
#: {{syn|en|invert|overturn|forset|tip|tip over|tip up|turn over|turn upside down|upend}}
#* '''1924''', W. D. Ross translator, [[w:Aristotle|Aristitle]], ''[[w:Metaphysics|Metaphysics]]'', Book 1, Part 9, [http://www.classicallibrary.org/aristotle/metaphysics/ The Classical Library], Nashotah, Wisconsin, 2001.
#*: ''But this argument, which first Anaxagoras and later Eudoxus and certain others used, is very easily '''upset'''; for it is not difficult to collect many insuperable objections to such a view.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To defeat unexpectedly.
#: {{syn|en|upend}}
#: {{ux|en|Truman '''upset''' Dewey in the 1948 US presidential election.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be upset or knocked over.
#: {{ux|en|The carriage '''upset''' when the horse bolted.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=[T]he locomotive exploded and '''upset''', and was completely wrecked.|work=The Locomotive|date=1 January 1880|page=9|issue=1|volume=1|publisher=The Hartford Steam Boiler Inspection And Insurance Company|location=Hartford, Conn.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[set up]]; to put [[upright]].
#: {{syn|en|redress|right|stand}}
#* R. of Brunne
#*: with sail on mast '''upset'''
# To thicken and shorten, as a heated piece of iron, by hammering on the end.
# To shorten (a [[tire]]) in the process of resetting, originally by cutting it and hammering on the ends.
# {{lb|en|transitive|basketry}} To support with an upset {{gl|type of woven row}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Thomas Okey|title=An Introduction to the Art of Basket-Making|page=32
|passage=Very coarse work is sided up without an [[upsett]], or is '''upsetted''' with a pair instead of a wale.}}

